/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Moist air is a mixture of dry air and water vapour.
   - Y is the absolute humidity in kg water/kg dry air; pA is the partial pressure of water vapour in kPa; P is the total pressure in kPa; 0.622 = 18.02/28.97, the ratio of the molar mass of water to that of air.
   - The same ratio comes from Dalton's law.
   - At a given temperature there is a maximum vapour pressure the air can hold: the saturation pressure pAS, read from steam tables.
   - Ys is the saturation humidity in kg water/kg dry air at that temperature; pAS is the vapour pressure of water at that temperature in kPa.
   - Relative humidity (RH) is a percentage.
   - RH is the relative humidity in per cent; pA is the actual vapour pressure in kPa; pAS is the saturation pressure at the same dry-bulb temperature in kPa.
   - Percentage humidity (percentage saturation) compares humidities instead of pressures: 100 Y/Ys at the same temperature.
   - Saturation pressure rises fast with temperature: at 40 °C it is 7.38 kPa, at 12.7 °C about 1.48 kPa.
   - Dew point is the temperature at which the vapour present just saturates the air at constant pressure.
*/
