/* book exam traps: Reading relative humidity as the water content: at the same Y, warmer air has a lower RH, so 20 % RH at 40 °C holds more water than 20 % RH at 20 °C. | Using percentage humidity and relative humidity interchangeably. They are equal only at saturation, and differ noticeably for dry air. | Putting pA in the denominator of the humidity formula as P + pA, or using P = 1 atm without converting to the kPa of the steam tables. | Forgetting the dry-air basis: the humid heat and enthalpy are per kg dry air, not per kg of moist air. | Mixing dew point with wet-bulb temperature: wet-bulb is an adiabatic saturation value, dew point is a saturation-from-vapour-pressure value, and they coincide only at 100 % RH. */
