/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The horizontal axis is dry-bulb temperature T in °C.
   - Curves of constant relative humidity run below the saturation curve.
   - h is moist-air enthalpy in kJ/kg dry air; T is dry-bulb temperature in °C; Y is humidity in kg water/kg dry air.
   - One chart is valid for one total pressure, usually 1 atm, and for air–water only.
   - Processes are paths.
   - Do not read enthalpy by moving horizontally.
   - Two air streams that mix without heat or work loss give a point on the straight line joining their states.
   - Ymix is mixture humidity; YA and YB are stream humidities; mA and mB are dry-air mass flows, all in consistent units.
   - The point divides the line in inverse proportion to the dry-air flows.
   - In the worked example, 100 kg dry air/h at 30 °C and Y = 0.010 mixes with 50 kg dry air/h at 40 °C and Y = 0.020.
*/
