/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An adiabatic saturator is a well-insulated chamber.
   - Y is humidity of the entering air (kg water/kg dry air); T is dry-bulb temperature (°C); Tas is adiabatic saturation temperature (°C); Ys(Tas) is saturation humidity at Tas; cs is humid heat ≈1.00+1.88Y kJ/(kg dry air·K); λs is latent heat 
   - The enthalpy balance says the sensible heat lost by the air equals the latent heat used to evaporate the water.
   - A wet-bulb thermometer has a bulb covered by a wet cloth wick.
   - hc is convective heat transfer coefficient (W/m²·K); Tw is wet-bulb temperature (°C); kY is mass transfer coefficient (kg/m²·s·ΔY); λ is latent heat at Tw (J/kg water); Ys(Tw) is saturation humidity at Tw; Y is humidity of the air.
   - For a reliable reading, keep the wick wet, keep air speed above about 3 m/s, and shield the wick from radiation.
   - The Lewis relation for air–water is hc/(kYcs) ≈ 1, in consistent units.
   - On the chart, start at the saturation curve at Tw.
   - The wet-bulb temperature is the lower limit of adiabatic cooling for air–water.
*/
