/* book exam traps: Writing wet-bulb temperature equal to adiabatic saturation temperature for every vapour. They are equal only for air–water, where the Lewis relation holds. | Using the dry-bulb temperature instead of Tw or Tas when finding Ys. The saturation humidity must be taken at the wet-bulb or adiabatic saturation temperature. | Mixing units: λ is in kJ/kg while cs is in kJ/(kg·K). Using λ in J/kg without converting the other term gives a factor-of-1000 error. | Forgetting the practical conditions. A dry wick, low air speed or strong radiation makes the wet-bulb reading unreliable. | Getting the sign wrong on the adiabatic line. As T falls, Y rises, so the slope is negative: −cs/λs. */
